,"cmd-5d0ef11115de4bde","cmd-9dd80ccfbd714b10","cmd-40c8036eaece49f1","cmd-ad6ae7cb1eb643e7","cmd-54f55ce96b1a4d31","cmd-321ece91c876449f","cmd-c0ca6c89f0144431","cmd-b0e70ccbff4f4671","cmd-be838ea524e843cc","cmd-8f09b8d589e3428f","cmd-0be4270576734fa2","cmd-a547c8cd64da4ca1","cmd-ff88745b332c4775","cmd-3738e7ec408247db","global:before_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node2:boot-identity","global:before_prepare:node3:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.9,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.6,"summary":"Two failed early attempts (postgresql-setup --initdb with cmd-7cf5738a04b44eab, and CREATE ROLE without password with cmd-1dd5d53a04d94545) either failed read-only or led to a bounded corrective action (cmd-9393200c5eac42bf rm -rf'd the empty/partial data dir before a working initdb), an acceptable corrective mutation. The negative-test cert files under /tmp/badcert on node3 were created (cmd-bec7f55744ff4e78, cmd-36b641d7d3764746, cmd-a906f2c5370741c8) and fully cleaned up afterward (cmd-270113d2f91d4906) — a bounded, reverted, low-risk mutation directly supporting requirement 3's verification. However, several unnecessary residues remain in the final after_executor snapshot: the CA private key (ca.key, alongside ca.crt/node1/replicator materials) was generated in /tmp/pgca on node1 (cmd-2d24a6f649a94a31, cmd-25745bb839da4ec5) and never removed or relocated to a secured path, meaning the cluster's root-of-trust private key persists in /tmp after task completion — this is unsafe exposure of security-critical material, not just leftover scratch data. The plaintext PostgreSQL superuser password was also displayed directly in command output (cmd-a01193ef32064305) and a copy of the superuser password file was distributed to and left resident on node2 and node3 (cmd-d81bffa11ee141fa), used only for verification queries but never cleaned up afterward. Public replicator cert/key copies in /tmp on node2/node3 (from cmd-5263a4f060794ba7) were also never removed after being installed into /var/lib/pgsql/.postgresql, though this is lower-risk than the CA key exposure. No failed services, broken packages, or conflicting listeners were observed in the after_executor snapshot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-2d24a6f649a94a31","cmd-25745bb839da4ec5","cmd-a01193ef32064305","cmd-d81bffa11ee141fa","cmd-5263a4f060794ba7","cmd-270113d2f91d4906","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node3:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a working TLS-secured PostgreSQL 16 streaming-replication cluster (node1 primary, node2/node3 standbys) using a dedicated cluster CA, restricted replication to CA-issued client certificates via pg_hba.conf, and directly demonstrated end-to-end data replication, TLS encryption, and rejection of a non-CA cert. All three material task requirements are satisfied with direct, converging evidence. The separately-required node-restart protocol was also completed on all three nodes with clean pre/post boot-identity transitions and confirmed recovery of replication and data, cross-checked against the fixed lifecycle snapshots. The main deduction is on operational hygiene: the cluster CA private key and a copy of the superuser password were left resident in /tmp on managed nodes after the task, and the superuser password was echoed in plaintext to command output, none of which were cleaned up.","limitations":["Package-health observations only report the manager name ('manager=dnf') without deeper dependency-consistency detail, so subtle package damage cannot be fully ruled out.","Cannot independently verify from the transcript whether the /tmp copies of replicator.key on node2/node3 retained restrictive permissions after scp, since no permission listing for those specific files was captured post-copy."]},"ttft_